type Settings = {
  currency?: string;
  freeShippingThreshold?: number;
  discountRate?: number;
  features?: { giftWrap?: boolean };
};

function resolveSettings(input: Settings) {
  return {
    currency: input.currency || 'USD',
    freeShippingThreshold: input.freeShippingThreshold || 50,
    discountRate: input.discountRate || 0.1,
    giftWrap: input.features?.giftWrap || false,
  };
}

console.log(resolveSettings({
  currency: 'ARS',
  freeShippingThreshold: 0,   // free shipping on every order
  discountRate: 0,            // launch week: no discount
  features: { giftWrap: false },
}));

setTimeout(() => {
  console.log('1');
  Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('2'));
}, 0);
console.log('3');
(async function () {
  console.log('4');
  await null;
  console.log('5');
})();
new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  console.log('6');
  setTimeout(() => console.log('7'), 0);
  reject('8');
})
  .then(() => console.log('9'))
  .catch(() => console.log('10'));

// previous candidate: 3 4 5 1

function display(baseObject) {
  const res = baseObject; // "copy" of the argument
  res.age = 12;
  return res;
}

const a = { name: 'Josh', age: 10, address: { city: 'Rosario' } };
const b = display(a);

console.log(a.age);    // ?
console.log(b.age);    // ?
console.log(a === b);  // ?

scenarios.modelAnswer:
**Output: `12`, `12`, `true`.**

**Why.** `const res = baseObject` copies the value of the reference, not the object: `a`, the parameter `baseObject`, `res` and the returned `b` are four names for one object. `res.age = 12` writes a property on that shared object, so every name observes 12, including the consumer that only held `a`. This is aliasing, and `const` is irrelevant to it — `const` locks the binding, never the value. Function arguments follow the same rule: JavaScript passes the reference by value, so reassigning the parameter (`baseObject = {...}`) would be invisible outside, while mutating a property is not.

**Fix 1, shallow.**

```js
function display(baseObject) {
  return { ...baseObject, age: 12 };
}
```

A new outer object; `a` keeps 10, `b` is 12, and `a !== b`. `Object.assign({}, baseObject)` is the same thing in different syntax. What it does not protect: nested values stay shared, so `res.address.city = 'Cordoba'` still mutates `a.address`. That is the right tool when the update touches only top-level properties.

**Fix 2, deep.**

```js
function display(baseObject) {
  const res = structuredClone(baseObject);
  res.age = 12;
  return res;
}
```

Nested objects get new identities, and `Date`, `Map`, `Set`, `RegExp`, `ArrayBuffer` and cycles survive. Its limits: functions, DOM nodes and symbols throw `DataCloneError`, and class instances come back as plain objects, losing their prototype. The JSON round-trip (`JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(baseObject))`) is the older deep clone and a worse one: functions, `undefined` and `Symbol` keys vanish, `Date` becomes a string, `NaN` and `Infinity` become `null`, and `Map`/`Set` become `{}`. Pick deep only when nested mutation is actually possible; copying the whole tree on every render is a cost with no benefit if only one level changes.

**Immutable `nick`.**

```js
Object.defineProperty(a, 'nick', {
  value: 'Joshie',
  writable: false,
  configurable: false,
  enumerable: true,
});

a.nick = 'other';  // sloppy: silently ignored — strict/module: TypeError
delete a.nick;     // sloppy: false and nothing happens — strict: TypeError
```

`writable: false` blocks reassignment, `configurable: false` blocks deleting and redefining the descriptor; with only `writable: false` the property could still be reconfigured back to writable. `enumerable: true` is what keeps it visible to `Object.keys` and `for...in`. The property must be defined while the object is still extensible, so before any freeze.

**`Object.freeze` versus `Object.seal`.** `Object.freeze(a)` makes the object non-extensible and sets every own property to non-writable and non-configurable: `a.age = 20` fails, `a.newProp = 1` fails, `delete a.age` fails (silent in sloppy mode, `TypeError` in strict). It is shallow — it changes the flags of `a`'s own properties, and `address` is one of those properties, so `a.address` cannot be replaced but `a.address.city = 'Cordoba'` still mutates the shared nested object. A deep freeze needs a recursive walk over the values. `Object.seal(a)` is the weaker half: non-extensible and non-configurable, but `writable` stays true, so `a.age = 20` succeeds while adding or deleting properties fails. Seal is enough to fix a shape, never enough to protect a value.

**The habit to state out loud.** Return a new object from anything that computes a new version, and never write through a parameter you were given. In a UI this is the same rule as state: a mutation keeps the reference and the update is invisible.

